    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "The heap and stale entries",
      "question": "In the heap version, why check whether the popped distance is larger than dist[u]?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. In the heap version, why do you check whether the popped distance is larger than the recorded distance? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Why check <em>d &gt; dist[u]</em> after popping?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Here is the standard implementation. Distances start at infinity, and the source at zero. The priority queue is a min-heap, so it needs greater. Pop the closest pair. If its distance is larger than the recorded one, it is a stale entry, so skip it. Otherwise relax each edge, and push the improved neighbor.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Dijkstra <em>with a min-heap</em>",
            "file": "dijkstra.cpp",
            "tag": "O((V + E) log V)",
            "size": 21,
            "width": 1100,
            "code": "vector<int> dijkstra(int n, const Graph& adj, int src) {\n    vector<int> dist(n, INF);\n    dist[src] = 0;\n    priority_queue<pair<int,int>, vector<pair<int,int>>, greater<>> pq;\n    pq.push({0, src});\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto [d, u] = pq.top(); pq.pop();\n        if (d > dist[u]) continue;  // stale entry\n        for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {\n            if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + w;\n                pq.push({dist[v], v});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dist;\n}",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "infinity",
                "lines": "2-3",
                "note": "INF everywhere, 0 at the source"
              },
              {
                "at": "greater",
                "lines": "4",
                "note": "greater<> makes it a min-heap"
              },
              {
                "at": "closest",
                "lines": "7",
                "note": "Pop the smallest (distance, vertex)"
              },
              {
                "at": "stale",
                "lines": "8",
                "note": "An older, longer entry: skip it"
              },
              {
                "at": "relax",
                "lines": "9-12",
                "note": "Relax and push the improvement"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "statement",
          "say": "There is no decrease-key in the standard heap, so a vertex can be pushed several times. The outdated copies are simply skipped when they surface. That is lazy deletion.",
          "props": {
            "text": "Push duplicates, <em>skip the stale</em>.",
            "sub": "std::priority_queue has no decrease-key, so outdated (distance, vertex) pairs stay in the heap until popped—then the check discards them.",
            "subAt": "outdated"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
